<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Seven Sages of Greece</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">7th–6th century BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/7th-century-bce">7th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Seven Sages of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a> were statesmen and philosophers of the 7th and 6th centuries BCE honoured in Greek tradition for their wisdom and maxims. Lists varied, but usually included <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thales-of-miletus">Thales of Miletus</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/solon">Solon</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bias-of-priene">Bias of Priene</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pittacus">Pittacus of Mytilene</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cleobulus">Cleobulus</a> of Lindos. Sayings such as &quot;know thyself&quot; were attributed to them.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/seven-sages-of-greece.jpg" alt="Seven Sages of Greece" width="320" height="278" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Clemens Schmillen · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:NMBSevenSagesMosaicBaalbekRectified.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Seven Sages, or Seven Wise Men, were an honorary title given to seven philosophers, statesmen and lawgivers of the 7th and 6th centuries BCE who were especially noted for their wisdom. The oldest explicit mention of a standard list of seven is in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plato">Plato</a>'s Protagoras. There <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/socrates">Socrates</a> names <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thales-of-miletus">Thales of Miletus</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pittacus">Pittacus of Mytilene</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bias-of-priene">Bias of Priene</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/solon">Solon</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cleobulus">Cleobulus</a> of Lindos, Myson of Chenae and Chilon of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sparta">Sparta</a>. He says they emulated Spartan education and made the brief remarks Know thyself and Nothing too much their joint first fruits of wisdom to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apollo">Apollo</a> at Delphi. The passage is elaborately ironical, so it is unclear which parts are meant seriously.</p><p>Diogenes Laertius reports that seven men were held in high esteem for wisdom well before <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plato">Plato</a>. According to Demetrius Phalereus, they first became known as the wise men in the archonship of Damasias, 582/81 BCE, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thales-of-miletus">Thales</a> the first so acknowledged.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The members were various. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thales-of-miletus">Thales</a> is the first well-known Greek philosopher, mathematician and astronomer. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pittacus">Pittacus</a> governed Mytilene on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lesbos">Lesbos</a> with the support of the common people. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/solon">Solon</a> framed laws that shaped <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athenian-democracy">Athenian democracy</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cleobulus">Cleobulus</a> was tyrant of Lindos, and Chilon was a Spartan politician credited with the militarisation of Spartan society.</p><p>Diogenes Laertius points out that his sources disagreed greatly about who belonged. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/periander">Periander of Corinth</a> or Anacharsis the Scythian was often put in the place of Myson. Plutarch says some counted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/epimenides">Epimenides</a> the seventh if they would not admit Periander. Dicaearchus gave ten names, Hippobotus twelve and Hermippus seventeen, from which people chose seven. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aesop">Aesop</a> was also a popular contender.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>Later tradition ascribed a pithy saying to each sage, though ancient and modern scholars have doubted this. A collection of 147 maxims inscribed at Delphi was preserved by Stobaeus as Sayings of the Seven Sages, but they were most likely popular proverbs later attributed to individuals. Stories told of a golden tripod, or a bowl or cup, to be given to the wisest, which passed from sage to sage until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thales-of-miletus">Thales</a> or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/solon">Solon</a>, depending on the version, dedicated it to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apollo">Apollo</a>. Diogenes Laertius also records that the maxims were thought sceptical and the sages precursors of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pyrrho">Pyrrho</a>'s scepticism, and that Dicaearchus held the seven to be merely shrewd men who had studied legislation.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Seven Sages of Greece” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the 6th century BCE Cleobulus, counted among the <b>Seven Sages</b>, ruled as tyrant and rebuilt the temple of Athena.</blockquote><a href="/state/lindos">Lindos</a></li><li><blockquote>Aristocratic feuds and tyrants such as Myrsilus marked the 7th century BCE, ended when Pittacus, one of the <b>Seven Sages</b>, ruled as elected aesymnetes c. 590 to 580 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/state/mytilene">Mytilene</a></li><li><blockquote>In the 6th century BCE Bias of Priene, counted among the <b>Seven Sages</b>, advised the Ionians to emigrate to Sardinia rather than submit to Persia.</blockquote><a href="/state/priene">Priene</a></li></ul></section>
